<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quedlinburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quedlinburg is a town on the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A favoured residence of the Ottonian kings, it was the burial place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, whose widow Matilda founded an abbey of noble canonesses there in 936; its abbesses ruled the town as princesses of the Empire until 1803. Its collegiate church, castle hill and hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed houses are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quedlinburg lies just north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> mountains, mostly in the western part of the valley of the Bode, a river that flows from the Harz into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. The town centre stands about 123 metres above sea level. Quedlinburg was the capital of its district until 2007, when the district was dissolved, and it has belonged to the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-reunification">German reunification</a> in 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A settlement called Gross Orden stood on the eastern bank of the Bode by the early 9th century, and Quedlinburg was first mentioned as a town in 922, in a donation by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. According to legend, Franconian nobles offered Henry the German crown there in 919. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s death in 936 his widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-ringelheim">Saint Matilda</a>, founded a community of women on the castle hill, where daughters of the higher nobility were educated and whose main task was to pray for Henry and his successors.</p><p>In 973 an imperial assembly was held at the court, at which Mieszko, duke of the Polans, Boleslav, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and nobles from as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> paid homage to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>. In 994 Otto III granted the town the rights of market, tax and coinage. Quedlinburg joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1426.</p><p>The abbey often disputed the town's independence, and in 1477 Abbess Hedwig expelled the forces of the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, which had supported the town. The town then left the League and was protected by the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Town and abbey became Lutheran in 1539. In 1697 Frederick Augustus I of Poland-Saxony sold his rights to the Elector Frederick III of Brandenburg for 240,000 thalers. After the abbey was secularised, Quedlinburg passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its abbesses, who were free to marry, ruled the town until then; the last was the Swedish princess Sofia Albertina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old town, with about 2,000 half-timbered houses, is among the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The oldest, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed Ständerbau, dates from 1347. The castle hill, the collegiate church of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Servatius</a>, a prime example of German Romanesque style, and the old town have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1994. The church's treasure, stolen by an American soldier, was returned to Quedlinburg in 1993. The poet Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock was born in the town, which has more than 24,000 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quedlinburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings endowed monasteries and convents such as <b>Quedlinburg</b>, Gandersheim and Corvey, which became centres of learning: the canoness Hrotsvitha of Gandersheim wrote Latin plays and poems in the tenth century, and the monk Widukind of Corvey wrote a history of the Saxons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After King Henry the Fowler died in 936 and was buried at <b>Quedlinburg</b>, his widow Matilda and their son Otto I founded a community of canonesses there to pray for him.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-quedlinburg">Imperial Abbey of Quedlinburg</a></li></ul></section>
